---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import RigDemo from "../components/RigDemo.astro";
---
<Base
  title="no.fail — deterministic control, verified judgment"
  description="no.fail installs AI into established businesses with a checker after every step. If we can't help you, you don't pay."
>
  <section class="nf-section">
    <div class="nf-content">
      <h1>Nothing checked the work.</h1>
      <p>
        That's why the pilot stalled — not your team, not the model. no.fail installs AI
        with a checker after every step, and the line stops at the first defect.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="nf-section">
    <div class="nf-content nf-content--wide">
      <p class="nf-eyebrow">The demo</p>
      <p>
        This is a demonstration of the mechanism, not a live AI run. Pick a scenario and
        watch what the line does with it.
      </p>
      <RigDemo />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="nf-section">
    <div class="nf-content">
      <h2>Method</h2>
      <p>
        The work runs in numbered steps. Each step is reviewed at the boundary before
        the next one starts. Nothing gets built three steps past the point where it went
        wrong, because there is no third step until the first one is signed off. We call
        it rigoka: stop-the-line discipline for AI. When a step is judged bad, the run
        stops there and what it produced stays exactly where it landed, so you diagnose
        from the actual thing rather than a summary of it. A stop is information.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>
        This site was built and verified the same way, on the pipeline architecture
        running in the demo above: every step gated before it started, every step's
        output checked after it finished.
        </b>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="nf-section">
    <div class="nf-content">
      <h2>What you get</h2>
      <p>
        Checking is structural. The step that does the work never grades the work, and
        the check comes from somewhere the work cannot reach.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every check leaves evidence. Each step records what it did, what it cost, and
        what was decided about it, as a byproduct of running. If you carry compliance
        exposure, that record is what procurement asks for.
      </p>
      <p>
        Failure has a price ceiling. A run stops at the defect, so the worst case is one
        step's budget and a readable reason.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="nf-section">
    <div class="nf-content">
      <h2>Terms</h2>
      <p>
        Discovery is a $1,000 working session, non-refundable, credited against your
        invoice if you engage. It ends with a written findings memo: what we'd automate
        first, where your process splits into stages, what we'd verify, roughly what it
        costs. It exists to filter people shopping for a silver bullet.
      </p>
      <p>If we can't help you, you don't pay.</p>
      <p>
        Done-for-you: we build it, we hand it over, and your team is walked into the
        system by a person rather than a login and a document.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="nf-section">
    <div class="nf-content">
      <h2>Fit</h2>
      <p>
        Not for freelancers and solo operators, for businesses that aren't established
        yet, or for anyone looking for a silver bullet.
      </p>
      <p>
        For you if you have revenue, staff, and a process everyone already knows should
        be automated. That is the work.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="nf-section">
    <div class="nf-content">
      <h2>What to do next</h2>
      <p>Tell us what your team does by hand that it shouldn't.</p>
      <a class="nf-btn" href="/contact/">Start there</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
